  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nNorthwesterly upper-level flow has moved into much of the central \nand northern Plains as a cutoff low makes its way towards the Great \nLakes. This has allowed strong surface ridging to move into much of \nthe central and northern US and ushered in below-average \ntemperatures with it. Stratus continues to fill in across eastern \nKansas and will persist for much of the morning today that should \nagain help to keep temperatures in to upper 60s to mid 70s by this \nafternoon. The low-level ridge axes begin to slide east of Kansas by \nWednesday, returning southerly surface flow. Subtle WAA and drier \nair with this change should act to clear skies a bit earlier \nWednesday morning and afternoon with highs a touch warmer, topping \nout in the mid to upper 70s - a pleasant day all around! \n\nThe first wave of several end of the week precipitation \nchances builds in by Wednesday night into Thursday as moisture and \nvorticity rounds the southern Plains upper ridge. Best forcing and \nmoisture profiles will reside across western and central Kansas so \nthat is where the best chances will be to see widespread \nprecipitation Wednesday PM into Thursday AM. By Thursday afternoon \ninto Friday, mid-level troughing moving in from the west begins to \nshunt the southern Plains ridge south and east. This should push PVA \nfurther east into eastern Kansas. A decent stream of gulf moisture \nin the 850mb layer will remain open to the central Plains into the \nweekend. PWATs during this period will hover around 1.75\", \nconfirming the overall quality and depth of moisture moving into \nKansas. With several waves passing over the plains Thursday through \nSaturday, PoPs will remain fairly high each day, maxing out in the \n50-70% range each day. A more stout shortwave moves through Saturday \nPM that should push a cold front across the area. Guidance quickly \nmoves this east of the area by Sunday, ushering in drier weather and \na compact mid-level ridge that could return afternoon temps back \ninto the mid to upper 80s. The compact ridge appears to persist \nacross the central US into Monday and possibly Tuesday. Southerly \nflow under this upper-pattern should promote slightly above-average \ntemperatures. Thankfully, long range guidance moves another trough \nin from the west by mid week that should return cooler weather and \nprecipitation chances.",
